<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · Balkans and Hungary</p><h1 class="st-title">Tsardom of Vidin</h1><p class="st-meta">1356 CE – 1396 CE (approx.) · capital Vidin</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Western Bulgarian realm of Ivan Sratsimir on the Danube, which split from Tarnovo in 1356 and was the last Bulgarian state to fall to the Ottomans.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tsar">Tsar</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-alexander-of-bulgaria">Ivan Alexander</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/second-bulgarian-empire">Second Bulgarian Empire</a> made his younger son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-shishman">Ivan Shishman</a> heir and gave his eldest, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-sratsimir">Ivan Sratsimir</a>, the north-western region around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidin">Vidin</a>. By 1356 Sratsimir ruled there as a separate tsar, and the two Bulgarian realms became rivals.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-sratsimir">Sratsimir</a> ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidin">Vidin's</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baba-vida">Baba Vida fortress</a> over lands along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, minting his own coins and placing his church under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ecumenical-patriarch-of-constantinople">Patriarch of Constantinople</a> rather than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliko-tarnovo">Tarnovo</a>. In 1365 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-i-of-hungary">Louis I of Hungary</a> seized Vidin and held it for four years, trying to convert the population to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catholicism">Catholicism</a>, until Wallachian and Bulgarian forces restored Sratsimir. Afterwards he became an Ottoman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1396 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-sratsimir">Sratsimir</a> opened <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidin">Vidin</a> to the crusaders of Sigismund of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hungary">Hungary</a>. After their defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nicopolis">Nicopolis</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bayezid-i">Sultan Bayezid I</a> took Vidin and Sratsimir was captured, ending Bulgarian independence until 1878.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/tsardom-of-vidin.jpg" alt="Tsardom of Vidin" width="320" height="264"><figcaption>Image: TRAJAN 117 This W3C-unspecified vector image was created with Inkscape . · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bulgaria_(14th_century).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1356 CE – 1396 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Vidin</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Ivan Sratsimir</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Middle Bulgarian</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/bulgaria">Bulgaria</a>, <a href="/countries/serbia">Serbia</a>, <a href="/countries/romania">Romania</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Vidin Tsardom, Principality of Vidin (Bulgarian)</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1376&amp;focus=tsardom-of-vidin" aria-label="Tsardom of Vidin on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5228 620 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Tsardom of Vidin lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5548 876L5619 905M5548 876L5446 783M5548 876L5709 970"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5619 905h0M5446 783h0M5709 970h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5548 876h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5548 876h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Vidin. <a href="/atlas?year=1376&amp;focus=tsardom-of-vidin">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
